the latest figures from the office for  national statistics show 36,000 591  people in the UK have died with  suspected or confirmed kovat 19 up to  the first of May now crucially the  number of new deaths fell by over two  thousand to six thousand six hundred and  seventy-six and this is the smallest  increase for a month now deaths are  falling in every setting including now  care homes where nine thousand seven  hundred and thirty nine people had died  by the first of May but care home deaths  make up an increasing share of the total  in the latest published week they  accounted for forty point four percent  of Cova deaths in England and Wales  fifty-nine percent in Scotland and 60s  and 1.7 percent of those registered in  Northern Ireland and it's not just  covert deaths that are alarming there  have been many more overall deaths than  normal in care homes and in people's  homes the worry with increased domestic  deaths is they might mean some people  aren't accessing care for other  conditions like heart attacks or strokes  now looking at where the care home  deaths are happening figures from the  Care Quality Commission suggests the  Hartford share Hampshire and Surrey have  recorded for most deaths in care homes  they've also got the largest populations  heading further north and County Durham  Sheffield and Lancashire also make the  top ten in England now together these  figures tell a story of falling death  tolls across the country but also one of  a care sector that remains under huge  pressure
 

